# Line endings are LF everywhere, in the index AND in the working tree.
#
# This file exists because the mismatch between those two cost three separate
# incidents in two days. core.autocrlf=true was rewriting files to CRLF on
# checkout while the index stayed LF (verified: 0 CRLF blobs in HEAD), and git
# reported the tree clean throughout, because it compared post-conversion.
#
# That is invisible until something reads the bytes rather than asking git.
# Both layout suites mutate their own source and match multi-line string
# literals against it, so a file that silently changed newline convention made
# a needle stop matching - and the suite then reported "the source mutation
# matched nothing, so it would have tested a clean build" rather than failing
# the assertion the reader expected. It broke in both directions: once when a
# text-mode write converted LF to CRLF, once when a git stash round-trip
# converted it back.
#
# eol=lf rather than -text on purpose. -text would freeze whatever is on disk,
# which would have preserved the corruption; eol=lf pins the working tree to
# what the index already holds, on every machine, whatever core.autocrlf says
# locally.
* text=auto eol=lf
